detailedExplanation.whatItIs:
The event loop juggles two queues with different priorities. Example:
console.log('1');
setTimeout(() => console.log('2'), 0);   // macrotask
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('3')); // microtask
console.log('4');
// Output: 1, 4, 3, 2
Microtask 3 runs before macrotask 2.

Inheritance via a chain of internal prototype links. Example:
function Animal(name) { this.name = name; }
Animal.prototype.speak = function() { return this.name + ' speaks'; };
const dog = new Animal('Rex');
dog.speak();   // found on Animal.prototype via the chain
dog's [[Prototype]] is Animal.prototype.

Generators produce iterators that yield values lazily. Example:
function* count() {
  yield 1;
  yield 2;
  yield 3;
}
const it = count();
it.next();   // { value: 1, done: false }
it.next();   // { value: 2, done: false }
Each next resumes the function until the next yield.

Contexts stacked as functions call each other. Example:
function first() { second(); }
function second() { third(); }
function third() { /* here, stack is: global, first, second, third */ }
first();
Each call adds a context; each return removes one.

Two module formats. Examples:
// ES Modules
import { foo } from './a.js';
export const bar = 1;

// CommonJS
const foo = require('./a.js');
module.exports = { bar: 1 };
The syntax and loading semantics differ.

A tail call returns a function call directly. Example:
// tail call: result returned directly
function f(n) { return g(n); }
// NOT a tail call: extra work after the call
function h(n) { return g(n) + 1; }
TCO would let recursive tail calls avoid stack growth.

Weak references that allow garbage collection. Example:
const cache = new WeakMap();
let obj = {};
cache.set(obj, 'metadata');
obj = null;  // entry can now be garbage-collected automatically
Unlike a regular Map, the entry does not keep obj alive.

Proxy intercepts operations; Reflect performs them. Example:
const target = { name: 'Ana' };
const proxy = new Proxy(target, {
  get(obj, prop) {
    console.log('reading', prop);
    return Reflect.get(obj, prop);
  }
});
proxy.name;   // logs 'reading name', returns 'Ana'

Immutability favors creating new data over mutating existing data. Example:
// mutating (avoid)
arr.push(4);
// immutable update (preferred)
const newArr = [...arr, 4];
const frozen = Object.freeze({ a: 1 });
frozen.a = 2;   // silently fails (or throws in strict mode)

FP composes pure, side-effect-free functions. Example:
// pure function
const add = (a, b) => a + b;
// composition and declarative style
const result = numbers.filter(isEven).map(double).reduce(sum, 0);
Data flows through functions without mutation.

Engines compile and specialize code at runtime. Conceptually:
// consistent shape: fast (same hidden class)
function Point(x, y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
// adding properties in different orders or later
// creates different hidden classes, slowing access
The engine tracks structure to optimize.

Two notions of equality for objects. Example:
const a = { x: 1 };
const b = { x: 1 };
const c = a;
a === b;   // false, different references
a === c;   // true, same reference
// deep equality of a and b would be true by value

Closures hold their captured scope alive. Example:
function makeHandler() {
  const bigData = new Array(1000000).fill('x'); // large
  return function() { /* uses something, but maybe not bigData */ };
}
const handler = makeHandler();
// bigData may stay in memory as long as handler exists

await splits a function around the event loop. Example:
async function f() {
  console.log('1');           // runs synchronously
  await null;                 // pause, schedule rest as microtask
  console.log('2');           // runs later as a microtask
}
f();
console.log('3');
// Output: 1, 3, 2

An IIFE returning a public interface over private state. Example:
const counter = (function() {
  let count = 0;                    // private
  return {
    increment() { count++; },       // public
    get() { return count; }
  };
})();
counter.increment();
counter.get();   // 1, count itself is inaccessible

Symbols are guaranteed-unique primitive values. Example:
const id = Symbol('id');
const id2 = Symbol('id');
id === id2;        // false, always unique
const obj = { [id]: 123 };
obj[id];           // 123, accessed only via the symbol
The description ('id') is just for debugging.
